<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mariano Ospina Rodríguez</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1805">1805</a>–<a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mariano Ospina Rodríguez was a Colombian politician, co-founder of the Conservative Party in 1849 and president from 1857 to 1861. The federal constitution of 1858, adopted under his government, created the Granadine Confederation. His attempts to strengthen central authority over the states led to the civil war in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-cipriano-de-mosquera">Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera</a> overthrew the government.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mariano-ospina-rodriguez.jpg" alt="Mariano Ospina Rodríguez" width="221" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mariano_Ospina_Rodr%C3%ADguez.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ospina was born in Guasca, in the Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-granada">New Granada</a>, on 18 October 1805, the son of Santiago Ospina and Urbina Rodríguez. Taught at home by his parents and then by José Félix Restrepo, he studied at the School of San Bartolomé in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> and took a law degree in 1827. In 1828 he joined the intellectuals and officers who opposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s assumption of dictatorial power, and on the night of 25 September 1828 he was among those who assaulted the Palacio de San Carlos in the failed coup known as the Noche Septembrina. He escaped to Antioquia, fought in 1829 in the army of General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-maria-cordova">José María Córdova</a> and hid in the mountains after Córdova was killed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1841 Ospina entered the cabinet of President Pedro Alcántara Herrán, first as Secretary of the Interior and then as Secretary of State, and in 1842 he carried out a comprehensive plan to expand the resources and programmes of the universities. He was elected governor of Antioquia in 1845 and again in 1854, and sat in Congress from 1849. With José Eusebio Caro he wrote the ideological platform of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservative-party-uk">Conservative Party</a> and founded the newspaper La Civilización, and he was one of the Conservative leaders in the civil war of 1851, which they lost. In the election of 1857 he defeated two Liberals, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-cipriano-de-mosquera">Tomás Cipriano de Mosquera</a> and Manuel Murillo Toro.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Imprisoned by Mosquera after his presidency, Ospina escaped with his brother Pastor in 1862 and in 1863 reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>, which granted them asylum. On the Pacific piedmont of Costa Cuca the family founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantation Las Mercedes, known as one of the best managed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, and ran a Scientific and Industrial College.</p><p>He had experimented with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> at Fredonia, Antioquia, since 1835, and after returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> in October 1871 he promoted its cultivation among farmers. In 1880 he established the Escuela Nacional de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Minas</a> de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medellin">Medellín</a> and the National Press published his manual Cultivo del Café. He died in Medellín on 11 January 1885 and was buried in the San Pedro Cemetery. His son Pedro Nel Ospina and his grandson Mariano Ospina Pérez both became president.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mariano Ospina Rodríguez” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The constitution of 1858, under the conservative president <b>Mariano Ospina Rodríguez</b>, created the Granadine Confederation, a union of eight states with a weak central government in Bogotá.</blockquote><a href="/state/granadine-confederation">Granadine Confederation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Guasca, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. Died in Cali, in what is now <a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=4.87,-73.88&amp;year=1857" aria-label="Guasca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mariano Ospina Rodríguez"><image href="/maps/globes/g30000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3829 4584h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3829 4584h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=4.87,-73.88&amp;year=1857">What was here in 1857? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
